# `eol=lf`, not just `text=auto`.
#
# The repository content is already LF and always was — `text=auto` sees to
# that on the way in. What it does not control is the way out: the working
# tree is left to each machine's git config, and `core.autocrlf=true`, the
# Windows default, writes CRLF back. Prettier is configured `endOfLine: lf`
# and reads the working tree, so `npm run format:check` fails on a Windows
# clone for any file git has re-checked-out, while CI on Linux passes on
# exactly the same commit.
#
# A check that passes in one place and fails in another is worse than one that
# fails everywhere: it teaches people to ignore it. `eol=lf` makes the working
# tree LF on every platform whatever the local config, so the two agree.
#
# Every binary type below is exempted — `eol` would corrupt them.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary

*.webp binary
*.woff2 binary
